A||||12||phillip||collectability||||1||
Z||000000||phillip||04-12-2000||11:32 AM||||<BR>if i were to find a convertible or indy fiero how collectable are they really? i mean from the standpoint of making mods.say, if i found an indy and did an engine change or put in a big stereo system in, would it be some kind of sacriledge. i know no one could tell me what to do, but iguess i'm wondering about maybe some kind or potential investment loss. do you think it might ever be like owning a '53 vette?<P>someone look in the future and get back to me.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mwbackus||04-12-2000||12:42 PM||||I look at it more like the old model A and T buckets.  Sure they are worth big $$ when restored precisely to stock, but they are also worth alot or even more as a street rod with some hefty performance upgrades.  So, even if the Fiero does become a collectable, a completely stock example will not necessarily be worth more than one that has been elaborately modified. <P><BR>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||04-12-2000||08:11 PM||||Personally, I wouldn't want to do a bunch of mods to an Indy Fiero. There are plenty of other Fieros begging to be modified. Having said that, I have a 40 Chevy Coupe I've been streetrodding for years. It started out as a complete basket case that had been the previous owner's streetrod project. I get two responses: 1) Wow! Cool! Neat! 2) You've ruined it! How dare you do that to a classic! To some people, a modified car is more valuable, to others, a restored original is worth more. Beauty is in the eye of the beholder (or beer holder).||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-12-2000||08:29 PM||||I wouldn't mess up an Indy unless it was already a big mess. I'd probably keep a formula very close to stock. Anything else I wouldn't worry about.<P>That I'm aware of there is no such thing as a factory convertible Fiero. T top or sunroof are the only RPO's. So if you had one it's already been modified. Do whatever you want to it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||04-13-2000||03:00 AM||||Opinion Alert! Opinion Alert! This is only an opinion!<P>With heavy heart I say no Pontiac Fiero will every attain this mythical status of "Collectable." Those who have had their 88s on blocks and bring them out 10 years later with only 100 miles on them aren't exactly selling them like hotcakes for $20,000. Fieros are doomed to be the misunderstood excuse for a footnote car. Another mistake in innovation that GM threw over to the side. Fieros will be following very closely in the same tracks as the Chevy Corvair. How much are Corvairs selling for? Do you see a huge demand for these cars of ideas before their times? No. But ask the owners of these cars and you will see the same fierce pride and protectiveness that we feel. Collectors cars? Nope. They just love their cars and love to drive them. You find an Indy Car don't be afraid to make it yours. Just don't think that your fun will come with a profit.<P>This has been an opinion. Had this been a for real fact, you would have been instructed as to where to scoff and aim the flame guns. This is an opinion from the Cliff Pennock's Fiero Forum. Thank you.||||reg||9
Z||000005||RossT||04-13-2000||03:47 AM||||Wk brings up some very good points.  I actually bought my 100 mile 88gt last May.  The original owner did try to sell it for 20 grand for about a year. When he drastically lowered the price it cought my eye. I bought it not to put it up on blocks, but to drive one of the coolest cars around. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-13-2000||07:54 AM||||Actually, the Corvair example might not be a good one to use. I never cared for Corvairs thenor now, but they have a small, but loyal bunch of owners (not unlike Fieros). One of those "unsafe at any speed" cars (according to Ralph Nader) in original or restored condition sells for an outragious price. Yes, they have became collectibles, maybe not to mainstream car guys, but to some it is. Same with the Fiero, in some respects, the nicer more desirable Fieros have already sort of became collectible. Try pricing a mint 88 GT and see what I mean. Will it ever gain the status of a 57 Chevy or a 32 Ford? Probably not, but it will have a fierce, loyal, nearly cult-like following of fanatical owners (us!).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||04-13-2000||08:13 PM||||Hey, I learned to drive on a '63 Corvair Monza two door coupe with twin carbs and 4 on the floor.  It was black with black interior, with that air cooled 180 degree six.  It was my brother's first car that he drove into a parked car during a street drag.  Oh well that was a long time ago.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Cooter||04-13-2000||09:31 PM||||If you collect them, then they are collectable! I have 2 already, my buddy Bob has 2 also. <BR>As far as keeping it original, you cant win or loose. It is your car, make it what you want. I took a 60,000 mile 85 SE and made a fastback out of it. Because it is a 4 cylinder car, it will never have any real value to anyone other than me. Now I have started on my 88 GT. The car had the factory lumbar option (1 out of 400+ built with the option). Someone on the Forum needed a lumbar seat to put their car back original, I wanted a different set of seats and now we are both happy. My GT has way too many miles on it to ever be a collectable, so it will be worth more with the upcoming 3.4 and other performance mod's. I have already been flamed for the destruction of an 88GT, but I am doing what makes me happy and the wife mad. Do what you want!||||reg||1
Z||000009||TalShino||04-13-2000||11:32 PM||||should we really care what our cars are worth to other ppl? They are OUR cars, it should only matter what they mean to US.... Who cares what the rest of the world thinks? if they dont like OUR little car, thier lose. not ours...<P>Ray||||reg||1
Z||000010||frontal lobe||04-14-2000||12:06 AM||||You would have to find an extremely low mile model to be worth anything as a collectable.  Then if you did, you wouldn't to want to drive it or destroy the value.  So just get a car you like and make it the way you like it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||04-15-2000||09:21 AM||||phillip--I don't think I'd mess with an Indy or Formula, either like ogre said.<P>batboy--"Wow!  Cool!  Neat!"||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-15-2000||03:46 PM||||On the other hand does anyone here think an 86.5 GT is worth nearly $7000us? I sure done't. That's what some dwonk in philadelphia area is asking for one.<P>Years ago I knew a guy that had an original Corvair Spider. Yes folks. The first US full production turbocharged car. It was in beautifull condition. I hope it's still running some place.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-15-2000).]||||reg||1
